The category-research plugin bundles a skill, a standard-library Python CLI, and
a callable Python API. Another plugin can use those entry points directly.
Packaging follows https://agent-plugins.org/specification. Native installation
and execution in multiple harnesses still need verification.

Each source product/variant has its own folder with a broad product.json index,
original source artifacts where accessible, original downloaded images, and
immutable capture history. Unknown fields and raw source prices are retained.
Discovery catalogues, source failures, cached-text limitations, unavailable
products, and ingredient gaps remain inspectable. Source listing counts are not
deduplicated market-wide product counts or proof of exhaustive UK coverage.

Import a prepared collection through the plugin from the repository root:

    python3 -B plugins/category-research/cli.py import --input <collection.json> --output collections --download-images --workers 4

The CLI reports a run-report path. Subsequent imports append evidence and retain
earlier captures. The import contract is documented inside the plugin skill.
The legacy script scripts/archive_product_sources.py reproduces only the initial
five-product exploratory sample.

The canonical UK chocolate workflow has two dataset layers:

- Raw: data/collections/chocolate/uk preserves original product indexes,
  arbitrary source fields, immutable captures, source artifacts and images.
- Silver: data/silver/chocolate/uk combines exact within-seller deduplication,
  typed schema/vocabulary/unit standardization, price normalization, evidence
  review and model-input eligibility in one build.

Build silver directly from the raw collections root:

    python3 -B scripts/build_chocolate_silver.py --archive-root data/collections --output data/silver/chocolate/uk

Silver keeps different shops' listings unique and product brand separate from
seller role/identity. source-listings.jsonl retains every original capture in
canonical seller groups, and listing-aliases.jsonl maps raw folders to those
seller listings. products.jsonl applies the 103-attribute chocolate-schema-1
contract. Separate assertions, prices, training candidates, eligible model
inputs, a review queue, versioned contracts, quality reports and a manifest
complete the dataset. brand/, retail/ and unknown/ expose products, prices and
source listings by seller role. Preserved source/image bytes remain in raw.

An optional --reviews <reviews.json> uses chocolate-schema-reviews-1, documented
in docs/chocolate-schema.md. The initial pricing design uses log regular GBP per
100 g, 11 selected reviewed predictors, family-grouped validation and
preprocessing learned only from training rows. scripts/chocolate_model.py
provides validation, splitting, frozen encoding and interpretation helpers; it
does not fit a regression. Unreviewed facts and unresolved price/tax basis keep
current model inputs empty. Comprehensive extraction evaluation and model
validation remain outstanding.

See docs/chocolate-silver.md for each layer's responsibilities, outputs, evidence
resolution and reproducibility. Separate deduplication and standardization CLIs,
and the earlier cleanup, remain compatibility/diagnostic helpers; creating
persistent intermediate layers is not required for the canonical workflow.

The standalone category-processing plugin packages the full post-collection
methodology with its own runtime, five-contract profiles, stable seller UIDs,
processing ledger, grouped mapping-review batches, calling-harness skill and
model-preparation CLI. Chocolate tracks 103 attributes; the coffee starter
demonstrates a second category with explicit structured inputs. Copying the
package requires no repository sibling modules or collection-plugin install.
It targets Agent Plugins 1.0.0; native client installation and execution across
harnesses remain unverified.

Run its portable workflow from the repository root:

    python3 -B plugins/category-processing/cli.py process --archive-root data/collections --profile plugins/category-processing/profiles/chocolate --output data/silver/category-processing/chocolate/uk
    python3 -B plugins/category-processing/cli.py summarize --silver-root data/silver/category-processing/chocolate/uk --output data/review-packets/chocolate/uk
    python3 -B plugins/category-processing/cli.py prepare-model --silver-root data/silver/category-processing/chocolate/uk --output data/model-preparation/chocolate/uk

See docs/category-processing.md and the package skill for profile versions,
review examples, outputs and mapping changes. The process command rebuilds the
full snapshot; ledger comparisons record input/rule changes without providing an
incremental cache. Mapping improvements use evidence, versioned diffs, tests and
impact review within the current task, with no external dispatch or scheduler.
prepare-model requires reviewed eligible rows and at least two families; current
unreviewed chocolate data does not pass. It writes frozen training/validation
inputs and an encoder, and fits no regression. The original chocolate CLI and
schema remain compatible under their established versions.

Documentation changes with behavioral/schema/model changes in the same change.
AGENTS.md and docs/documentation-policy.md name the canonical documents and sync
conditions. Run the drift guard and relevant tests before finishing:

    python3 -B scripts/check_documentation.py
    python3 -B -m unittest discover -s scripts/tests -v
    python3 -B -m unittest discover -s plugins/category-processing/tests -v

.github/workflows/validation.yml runs the tests and documentation guard on pull
requests and pushes. docs/lifecycle/plan.md records implementation status,
verification commands, limitations, and remaining work.

Public dataset publication uses the category-independent text-evidence policy in
docs/spec.md, section 3.1.1. It includes product records, original text/HTML/JSON
evidence, capture histories, catalogues, discovery/run evidence, and coverage.
Image files and HTTP transfer caches remain local; their recorded references
are disclosed as omitted. Accepted source bytes are preserved in losslessly
compressed category/market bundles, with a product JSONL index and manifests.

Build and inspect the export without network access:

    python3 -B scripts/publish_collections.py

Publish the export to a public Hugging Face dataset using a cached login or
HF_TOKEN (uv installs the script's pinned client dependency):

    uv run --script scripts/publish_collections.py --upload --repo-id CoralLeiCN/rgc-collections

The exporter discovers all category/market studies under data/collections/.
Generated files under data/huggingface-export/ remain ignored by Git. No
recurring upload job is configured; the current publication is a one-time run.

All repository content must be written in English, regardless of prompt language.
